{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And you shall purge the innocent blood.</b> Rashi explained: if the murderer is found after the breaking of the heifer's neck, they do not exempt him. And he wondered, for in Ketubot chapter 11, Rabbi Levi concludes: 'ואתה תבער הדם הנקי' — those who shed blood were compared to the broken-necked heifer, to tell you: just as the broken-necked heifer is from the neck — i.e., to teach that a murderer is judged by the sword. And the Rav, our Master Moses, of blessed memory, explained that that sugya follows the one who says strangulation is severe, and so it implies in the chapter ארבע מיתות, where it derives that a murderer is by the sword from what is written: 'והבאתי עליכם חרב נוקמת נקם ברית', and concludes: we found a slave [killed] by the sword — from where do we know a free man? It is a fortiori: if he killed a slave by the sword, a free man — all the more so. That works according to the one who says strangulation is lenient. But according to the one who says strangulation is severe, what is there to say — i.e., from where does it emerge that a free man is killed by the sword? For now there is no a fortiori. And it answers: it emerges from what is taught in a beraita: 'ואתה תבער הדם הנקי' — those who shed blood were compared to the broken-necked heifer: just as there, from the neck, so too here, from the neck. By implication, according to the one who says strangulation is severe, 'ואתה תבער' was needed to compare a murderer to the broken-necked heifer. And if the murderer is found after the breaking of the heifer's neck — for we hold that strangulation is lenient compared to the sword, so now we derive a murderer by the sword by a fortiori from a slave. And if you ask for him, 'ולארץ לא יכופר' etc. — what is it for? One can say: it was needed for perhaps the murderer is found after Yom haKippurim — they do not exempt him, even though there are both the breaking of the heifer's neck and Yom haKippurim. And the other one — perhaps he does not have this [difficulty]."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ולארץ לא יכופר' is Numbers 35:33; the phrase 'והבאתי עליכם חרב נוקמת' is Leviticus 26:25. The closing 'ואידך שמא לית ליה האי' is rendered hyper-literally as 'and the other one — perhaps he does not have this'; it refers to the first interpretation (Rashi's) not having this particular difficulty."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:16.829448890Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}